# WebGL 3D Engine: rebuilding cities to save lives

SOS Location v2.0 had to answer a simple question with an extremely difficult solution: *how do you give an operations commander a high-definition tactical view of an entire city, in real time, during a catastrophe?*

The answer was to build a **WebGL 2.0 engine from scratch**, using Three.js as the base and open data to reconstruct the urban mesh.

## Data sources for the 3D world

Before any shader, the real problem is data:

| Source | What it provides | API |
|---|---|---|
| OpenTopography (SRTMGL1) | Global 30m DEM | REST |
| OpenStreetMap / Overpass | 3D buildings, streets | Overpass QL |
| Open-Meteo | Real-time weather | REST |
| IBGE | Population datasets | REST |

The ingestion flow starts with an Overpass query that fetches every building inside a bounding box:

```typescript
const overpassQuery = `
  [out:json][timeout:60];
  (
    way["building"](${bbox});
    relation["building"](${bbox});
  );
  out body;
  >;
  out skel qt;
`;

const buildings = await fetchOverpass(overpassQuery);
const meshes = buildings.elements
  .filter(el => el.type === 'way' && el.nodes)
  .map(el => buildingToMesh(el, demElevation));
```

## Building the terrain

The DEM (Digital Elevation Model) is an elevation grid. The conversion to Three.js `PlaneGeometry` looks like this:

```typescript
function demToTerrain(dem: Float32Array, width: number, height: number): THREE.Mesh {
  const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(
    TILE_SIZE_METERS, TILE_SIZE_METERS,
    width - 1, height - 1
  );

  const positions = geometry.attributes.position.array as Float32Array;
  for (let i = 0; i < dem.length; i++) {
    // Z sits at index 2 of each vertex (x, y, z)
    positions[i * 3 + 2] = dem[i] * ELEVATION_SCALE;
  }
  geometry.computeVertexNormals();

  const material = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
    map: terrainTexture,
    displacementMap: demTexture,
    wireframe: false,
  });

  return new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
}
```

## 3D buildings with roofs

Each OSM building becomes an `ExtrudeGeometry`. The challenge is that the OSM polygon is 2D, while height comes from `building:levels` or `height` tags:

```typescript
function buildingToMesh(way: OsmWay, dem: DemProvider): THREE.Mesh {
  const coords = way.nodes.map(id => nodeMap[id]);
  const shape = new THREE.Shape(
    coords.map(c => new THREE.Vector2(c.lon * SCALE, c.lat * SCALE))
  );

  const levels  = parseInt(way.tags?.['building:levels'] ?? '2', 10);
  const height  = parseFloat(way.tags?.height ?? String(levels * 3.2));
  const baseElev = dem.getElevationAt(way.center.lat, way.center.lon);

  const geometry = new THREE.ExtrudeGeometry(shape, {
    depth: height,
    bevelEnabled: false,
  });

  const material = buildingMaterialByType(way.tags?.building || 'yes');
  const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
  mesh.position.z = baseElev * ELEVATION_SCALE;
  return mesh;
}
```

## Live disaster layers

What makes SOS Location different from a generic 3D viewer is the **real-time overlay of risk layers**. Each layer is backed by a custom shader.

### Flood shader (FragmentShader)

```glsl
uniform float u_waterLevel;
uniform float u_time;
uniform sampler2D u_elevation;

varying vec2 v_uv;
varying float v_elevation;

void main() {
  float distToWater = u_waterLevel - v_elevation;

  if (distToWater > 0.0) {
    // Water: wave animation driven by sin/cos
    float wave = sin(v_uv.x * 40.0 + u_time * 2.0) * 0.5
               + cos(v_uv.y * 40.0 + u_time * 1.7) * 0.5;
    float depth = clamp(distToWater / 5.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    vec3 waterColor = mix(vec3(0.1, 0.4, 0.8), vec3(0.0, 0.1, 0.5), depth);
    gl_FragColor = vec4(waterColor + wave * 0.05, 0.75);
  } else {
    discard;
  }
}
```

### Integrating layers into the pipeline

```typescript
class DisasterLayerManager {
  private layers = new Map<DisasterType, THREE.Mesh>();

  addLayer(type: DisasterType, params: LayerParams) {
    const geometry = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(TILE_SIZE, TILE_SIZE, 128, 128);
    const material = this.shaderForType(type, params);
    const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
    this.scene.add(mesh);
    this.layers.set(type, mesh);
  }

  update(deltaT: number, realTimeData: RealTimePayload) {
    this.layers.forEach((mesh, type) => {
      const mat = mesh.material as THREE.ShaderMaterial;
      mat.uniforms.u_time.value += deltaT;
      if (type === 'flood') {
        mat.uniforms.u_waterLevel.value = realTimeData.waterLevel;
      }
    });
  }
}
```

## Operational result

The engine renders full cities at 60 FPS in a modern desktop browser:
- **Curitiba (BR)**: ~12,000 buildings, 30m DEM, load time around 3s
- **Shimane (JP)**: validated with GSI / MLIT datasets
- Active layers: flood, fire, landslide and earthquake vibration (shader-based)

The next step, Phase 9, is the **Full WebGL City Component** with Keycloak-gated access for real command centers.

## Link to the project

This engine is the core of [SOS Location](/projects/sos-location/). The architecture posts explain how the data layers arrive here.

